WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.240
My name's Amy.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.240
Heineike, and I'm a research engineer at Tessl,

3
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.400
so that means I spend my days trying to work out what on earth LLMs are doing,.

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.000
I was really searching for a place where I'd get to spend

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.160
a lot of time really deeply understanding what was possible with AI.

6
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.840
And I felt like coding would be one of the most interesting domains to do that in.

7
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.560
I really like working on ambiguous problems

8
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.080
It’s not like there's necessarily a playbook for some of these things,

9
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.640
and you are definitely having to make a bet on something that you think

10
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.760
is going to be interesting without knowing for sure how it will play out.

11
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.320
We're in a space that's moving, you know, incredibly quickly.

12
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.040
It feels like a huge privilege to get to be in a place where you've got

13
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.400
enough resources that you can take a bit of a long view of it.

14
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.200
So on the one side, everything's going really fast

15
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.200
and we need to be out there quickly and responding to the world.

16
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.040
On the other hand, we can kind of think a little bit about

17
00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.680
where is this going and what will that mean for, customers?

18
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.640
As a researcher, I need really hard problems

19
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.680
and I need a bunch of compute, free tokens,

20
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.600
And then people that I can bounce ideas off of and, get feedback from.

21
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.040
And then I need customers, users who have real guidance on what's important.

22
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.440
We have all of those things in abundance.

23
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.600
As a company that there's some pretty ambitious ideas

24
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.160
about how much we want to grow and kind of seize the opportunity.

25
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.480
And I think with a startup, you are always betting that,

26
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.360
you know, as the company scales, that creates all kinds of opportunities

27
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.440
for new roles or new responsibilities.

28
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.760
We've got all the opportunity in the world there,

29
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.440
you know, we've got positioning right now, we've got capital at the right time.

30
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.920
We're in this incredibly hot and interesting space.

31
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.040
So we've got everything going for us to go just kind of seize a big opportunity.

32
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.080
And then create our own individual stories inside of that.
